Remote access
09-api-access
The page has four views, chosen with the keys 1 to 4. The doors view shows what REMOTE.API and REMOTE.SSH say with no keyword, which is what is installed, read back from sd.conf, the firewall rules and the ssh task. A changes the API and S changes ssh: each is off (no listener at all), local (it listens and the firewall lets only this computer in) or open (other computers on the network can connect). Open lets other computers reach this one, so its box wants the word OPEN typed. After the box the terminal is given to the verb, which asks for the administrator password again and has the doors helper, which the installer set up, make the change. Local or open sets ssh up if it was never set up. An API change to or from off takes effect when SD is next started (sd-solo -stop, then sd-solo -start, or the next boot); the firewall part and every ssh change take effect at once.
The servers view lists the SDNet servers of the netservers file: the name, the address, the remote account and mode, the pin shortened, and the local accounts that may use the server. X deletes the one under the cursor after a box. N asks for every field, checks them as SET.SERVER does, and gives the terminal to the verb, whose CONNECT step shows the key the server presents and asks whether to trust it.
The connected view lists every session by the door it came through, and the TLS view shows this computer's certificate and the hash of its public key, which another SD server pins. Each command shows what it does and runs only when you answer Run; Cancel is what Enter chooses. F5 reads everything again.
